            </div>

            <div class="notes-card card" id="tr-notes">
              <h4>Notes</h4>
              <p>
                <strong>Transition radiation.</strong> An electron crossing the boundary between two
                media radiates because its image charge is created or destroyed there&nbsp;[1,&nbsp;2].
                For a <em>semi-infinite</em> half space there is a single interface and only the
                backward lobe, emitted into the vacuum&nbsp;[2,&nbsp;3]. For a free-standing
                <em>film</em> of thickness&nbsp;d the entrance interface (B<sub>1</sub>), the film
                interior including Cherenkov radiation (B<sub>2</sub>) and the exit interface
                (B<sub>3</sub>) interfere, and light leaves on both sides&nbsp;[1,&nbsp;4].
                As d grows the film tends to the half-space result, which the
                <em>d&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;&infin;</em> overlay makes visible.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Angular pattern.</strong> The emission is p-polarized and vanishes both along
                the beam (&theta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;0) and in the surface plane (&theta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;90&deg;),
                so the lobe is a hollow cone about the trajectory. The <em>Angular</em> view draws the
                Ginzburg&ndash;Frank perfect-conductor limit&nbsp;[5] as a reference. A perfect mirror
                transmits nothing, so that limit exists for backward emission only: it is omitted on
                a film's forward side, where there is nothing for it to be the limit of.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Resonances.</strong> The two interface contributions add in phase when
                t&thinsp;&sigma;&nbsp;+&nbsp;t/&beta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;2&pi;(m&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&frac12;) with
                t&nbsp;=&nbsp;2&pi;d/&lambda; (Eq.&nbsp;3 of&nbsp;[4]); the orders m&nbsp;=&nbsp;1,&nbsp;2,&nbsp;3
                are drawn over the thickness map. <em>Terms</em> compares the full result with the
                B<sub>1</sub>+B<sub>3</sub> interface-only control.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Material.</strong> The <em>Material</em> view plots the permittivity the
                formulas above are actually being handed &mdash; Re&nbsp;&epsilon; and
                Im&nbsp;&epsilon; (dashed), or n and k &mdash; for the built-in tables, the Drude
                model, a custom &epsilon;, an uploaded table or a refractiveindex.info entry alike.
                It is read through the same accessor the kernels use, so what is drawn is what is
                computed; with no data loaded it says so instead of drawing a vacuum line.
              </p>
              <p class="theory-note">
                <strong>Model and assumptions.</strong> Normal incidence; a free-standing film in
                vacuum (no substrate); local, isotropic, linear permittivity &epsilon;(&omega;) with
                Im&nbsp;&epsilon;&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0; a classical dielectric response and a straight,
                non-recoiling electron trajectory. Begrenzung, surface roughness, finite beam size,
                bulk-plasmon losses and quantum recoil are not included. Probabilities are photons per
                electron per nm (or per eV) of the free-space wavelength, integrated over the
                collection cone, or per steradian where the axis says so.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
